PDL_SPECIFICATION_VERSION 0.2.0

STATUS DRAFT

SUPERSEDES PDL_SPECIFICATION_VERSION 0.1.0

SCOPE

Adds nested-section extraction on top of 0.1.0's flat grammar, for
documents like the Instance Contract that mix structural fields with
hand-wrapped prose. Does not replace 0.1.0 — flat key-value fields
(CONTRACT_VERSION, STATUS, OWNER, and similar top-of-file metadata)
still follow that grammar exactly.

WHY_NOT_FULLY_GENERIC

A fully generic indentation-based grammar cannot reliably tell a new
nested field apart from a wrapped continuation of the previous line's
value, when both use indentation for different purposes — one for
structure, one for visual line-wrap alignment. Rather than guess, this
version requires the reader to know the document's own small, fixed
vocabulary of top-level section names in advance — the same technique
the old system's BOOTSTRAP_CONTEXT.pdl already used via its own
READ_ORDER list.

TOP_LEVEL_SECTION_SPLITTING

Given a known list of section names, split the document into the
metadata before the first known name (flat key-value, per 0.1.0) and one
raw text block per named section, ending at the next known name or END.
The same technique applies recursively — a section's own raw body can be
split again by a known list of its own sub-entry names.

INNER_FIELD_EXTRACTION

Within a section's raw text, a field is a line whose indentation matches
that list's own established base indentation (e.g. every entry under
REQUIRED_FIELDS starts at the same column). Any line indented deeper
than that base is a continuation of the previous field's wrapped value,
not a new field — this is what makes hand-wrapped prose safe to mix with
structured fields without misparsing it.

END
